CAT = "sqeDispute"; SEG = "DIS"
Q = [
("A buyer sues a supplier for breach of a simple contract. When does the six-year limitation period start?",
 "On the date of the breach", ["When the breach is found", "When loss is first suffered", "When the contract was made"],
 "Under s.5 Limitation Act 1980 a contract claim must be brought within six years from the date the cause of action accrued, which is the breach. In negligence time usually runs from damage."),
("A client was injured in a road collision last month. What is the normal limitation period for her claim?",
 "Three years", ["Two years", "Six years", "Twelve years"],
 "Personal injury claims have three years from the injury or, if later, the date of knowledge (s.11 Limitation Act 1980)."),
("Before suing an individual for an unpaid trade invoice, which pre-action protocol must the business follow?",
 "Debt Claims Protocol", ["Personal Injury Protocol", "Professional Negligence Protocol", "Disrepair Protocol"],
 "The Pre-Action Protocol for Debt Claims applies where a business claims payment of a debt from an individual, including a sole trader."),
("A claim form is issued for service in England. By when must the claimant take the step required to serve it?",
 "Four months after issue", ["Within 14 days of issue", "Six months after issue", "Within 28 days of issue"],
 "CPR 7.5 requires the relevant step for service within the jurisdiction to be taken before midnight on the calendar day four months after issue. Six months applies to service abroad."),
("A defendant files an acknowledgment of service in time. When is the defence due?",
 "28 days after particulars", ["14 days after particulars", "21 days after issue", "42 days after issue"],
 "Under CPR 15.4 the defence is due 14 days after service of the particulars, or 28 days if an acknowledgment of service is filed."),
("A defendant files nothing in reply to a claim for a fixed sum. What may the claimant request?",
 "Default judgment", ["Summary judgment", "An interim payment", "Strike out of the claim"],
 "Where no acknowledgment or defence is filed in time, the claimant may obtain default judgment under CPR Part 12, by request for a specified sum."),
("On what basis may a court give a claimant summary judgment?",
 "No real prospect of a defence", ["Defence was filed late", "Claim is under £10,000", "Defendant missed a court hearing"],
 "CPR 24 allows summary judgment where the defendant has no real prospect of successfully defending the claim and there is no other compelling reason for a trial."),
("A defendant rejected a claimant's Part 36 offer and the claimant did better at trial. What can follow?",
 "Indemnity costs, enhanced interest", ["Defence struck out", "Costs capped at fixed sums", "Each party paying its own"],
 "Under CPR 36.17(4) the court will usually award indemnity costs and enhanced interest from expiry of the relevant period, plus an additional amount, unless unjust."),
("A claimant rejects a defendant's Part 36 offer, then recovers less at trial. Usual order for costs after the relevant period?",
 "Claimant pays those costs", ["Defendant pays all costs", "Each side bears its own", "Costs reserved to any appeal"],
 "Where a claimant fails to obtain a judgment more advantageous than a defendant's Part 36 offer, CPR 36.17(3) usually makes the claimant pay the defendant's costs from the end of the relevant period."),
("A Part 36 offer is made well before trial. What is the shortest relevant period it may give?",
 "21 days", ["14 days", "28 days", "7 days"],
 "A Part 36 offer made more than 21 days before trial must specify a relevant period of not less than 21 days for acceptance with costs protection."),
("A £60,000 contract claim needs a two-day trial and one expert for each side. Which track is normal?",
 "Intermediate track", ["Fast track", "Multi-track", "Small claims track"],
 "Under CPR 26.9 the intermediate track takes claims up to £100,000 that can be tried in three days or less with limited expert evidence. The fast track stops at £25,000 and one day."),
("A defendant is moving money offshore to defeat any judgment. Which interim order fits best?",
 "Freezing injunction", ["Search order", "Interim payment", "Specific disclosure"],
 "A freezing injunction stops a party dealing with assets where there is a good arguable case and a real risk of dissipation. A search order preserves evidence."),
("A defendant believes the claimant company is insolvent. What order protects her if she wins?",
 "Security for costs", ["Freezing injunction", "Interim payment", "Summary judgment"],
 "Under CPR 25 the court may order a claimant company to give security for costs where there is reason to believe it will be unable to pay the defendant's costs if ordered to."),
("Under standard disclosure, besides the documents it relies on, what must a party disclose?",
 "Those adverse to its case", ["Its privileged papers", "Drafts of its pleadings", "Documents it will not use"],
 "CPR 31.6 standard disclosure covers documents a party relies on and those which adversely affect its own case or adversely affect or support another party's case."),
("A solicitor obtains an expert report for a claim that is now expected. What privilege may protect it?",
 "Litigation privilege", ["Legal advice privilege", "Without prejudice privilege", "Public interest immunity"],
 "Communications with third parties made for the dominant purpose of litigation in reasonable contemplation attract litigation privilege. Legal advice privilege covers lawyer and client only."),
("A genuine settlement letter is headed without prejudice. Can the trial judge see it on liability?",
 "No, it is inadmissible", ["Yes, once trial starts", "Yes, if it was refused", "Yes, if either side asks"],
 "Genuine attempts to settle are protected by without prejudice privilege and cannot be shown to the court on liability, unless both parties waive it."),
("To whom does an expert witness owe an overriding duty in civil proceedings?",
 "To the court", ["To the paying party", "To the client's solicitor", "To the other experts"],
 "CPR 35.3 says an expert's duty is to help the court on matters within the expert's expertise, and this overrides any obligation to the person instructing or paying."),
("A judgment debtor has money in his bank account. Which enforcement method takes it directly?",
 "Third party debt order", ["Charging order", "Attachment of earnings", "Writ of control"],
 "A third party debt order requires a bank holding money for the debtor to pay it to the creditor. A charging order secures the debt on land or shares."),
("A party wants to appeal a district judge's final decision in the County Court. Who usually hears the appeal?",
 "A circuit judge", ["The Court of Appeal", "A High Court master", "Another district judge"],
 "Appeals from a district judge of the County Court generally go to a circuit judge, and permission to appeal is usually required."),
("A party refuses mediation in a claim suited to it. Can the court order the parties to mediate?",
 "Yes, if it is proportionate", ["No, it is voluntary only", "Only on small claims", "Only if both consent"],
 "Since Churchill v Merthyr Tydfil and the CPR changes in 2024, the court may order parties to engage in ADR if this does not impair the right to a hearing and is proportionate."),
]
